# modules.cpp platform MCU provider specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the implemented pure C++ platform-provider mechanism
for mm.mcu in release v1.2.0. status: official gives it the same authority as
the other implemented specifications in docs.

docs/modules-mm.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented manifest schema.
docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains authoritative for SDK and board selection,
responsibilities, and link ownership. docs/modules-build.mdy remains
authoritative for the implemented dependency closure and link pipeline.
docs/modules-c++20.mdy remains authoritative for permitted project code.

# Decision

platform-interface and platform-provider belong to source-manifest version mm:
1.2 and are part of release v1.2.0. An older binary rejects manifests using
them rather than assigning them an older meaning.

The portable interface remains the globally unique core module mm.mcu. Each
platform implementation is a different, globally unique provider module. A
selected SDK or board binds the interface to one provider. The build injects
that provider into an executable only when the executable's ordinary dependency
closure reaches mm.mcu.

An application declares only:

Opening fence
use: mm.mcu
Closing fence

It never names a board, SDK, vendor library, or provider module. Platform
selection supplies the provider exactly as it already supplies startup code,
runtime support, and link ownership.

The mechanism does not relax global module-name uniqueness. It does not make a
board source part of the module dependency graph. It does not expose a generic
mm_mcu C ABI as the portable interface.

# Required invariants

- mm.mcu has one meaning in the whole repository.
- Every provider module has a globally unique module name.
- One selected platform resolves an interface to exactly one effective
  provider, with a selected board overriding its selected SDK's default.
- An application neither names nor imports its provider.
- A provider is linked only when the executable closure requires its interface.
- An executable that does not use mm.mcu receives no mm.mcu provider object.
- A missing, duplicate, or unavailable provider is diagnosed before linking.
- Project and external CMake link paths receive the same augmented object list.
- The model exposes the binding without collapsing duplicate module names.
- Core applications remain core because the provider is supplied by the
  selected platform, not declared by the application.

# Why duplicate provider names are rejected

Several platforms must not export interchangeable modules all named lib.mcu.
The project loader rejects duplicate module names before lane filtering. That
invariant prevents index_of_module from selecting whichever declaration happens
to appear first in manifest walk order.

Weakening the check would make correctness depend on every availability rule
leaving exactly one declaration alive. If two survived, build and test could
silently select different implementations after an innocent folder reorder.
Model and document also load the unfiltered project and could expose an
arbitrary provider. A platform role therefore does not redefine module
identity.

Provider names identify implementations rather than roles. Examples are:

- platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
- platform.pico.mcu
- platform.mps2_an385.mcu

These names are explicit module identifiers, not values derived mechanically
from platform names. C++ module components are identifiers, so a platform name
containing a hyphen commonly uses an underscore in its provider module name:
rp2040-ram may bind platform.rp2040_ram.mcu. The platform-provider declaration
is the authoritative mapping; no tool infers it from either spelling.

One provider may be bound by several compatible boards or SDKs. Two different
providers may implement the same interface because their module names remain
different.

# Why board-source dependencies are insufficient

Board file entries are compiled as one synthetic board unit and their objects
are appended to every executable in the lane. They have no use declarations and
do not participate in order or closure traversal.

Allowing board sources to import modules would be a useful independent feature,
but it would not provide conditional implementation selection. A board source
that registers a Platform subclass refers to mm.mcu. Appending that object to an
executable whose closure does not contain mm.mcu creates undefined references.
Appending mm.mcu and the provider to every executable instead makes unused
applications pay for the interface.

The observed module-attached vtable and set_platform link failures demonstrate
that mismatch. They do not establish that a module-attached polymorphic class is
unusable. When an application reaches mm.mcu and the build also injects the
selected provider closure, both sides of those references are present.

# Manifest declarations

Two mm: 1.2 declarations express the relationship. docs/modules-mm.mdy lists
them in the closed key table and the shared manifest gate enforces their version
and kind placement in every manifest-reading tool.

## platform-interface

platform-interface is an optional marker valid only on kind: module. It occurs
at most once and must have no value. Its presence marks the manifest's module as
a portable interface that requires a selected platform provider at executable
link time. The module declaration already supplies the interface identity, so
the marker does not repeat that name or create a mismatch case.

The mm.mcu manifest declares:

Opening fence
mm: 1.2
kind: module
name: mcu
module: mm.mcu
platform-interface:
file: status.cppm mm.mcu:status
file: platform.cppm mm.mcu:platform
file: gpio.cppm mm.mcu:gpio
file: uart.cppm mm.mcu:uart
file: timer.cppm mm.mcu:timer
file: mcu.cppm mm.mcu
file: src/platform.cpp
Closing fence

The declaration does not make the module itself unavailable. mm.mcu can be
compiled, checked, modelled, and documented without selecting a platform. The
provider becomes a requirement only when linking an executable whose closure
contains the interface.

## platform-provider

platform-provider is repeated and valid on kind: sdk and kind: board. Each value
contains an interface module name followed by one provider module name:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
Closing fence

Both names are required. Extra fields are rejected. The interface must resolve
to a module carrying the platform-interface marker. The provider must resolve
to a different kind: module target, and its use closure must reach the interface
module.

All declarations are resolved after the complete manifest tree is collected,
so folder order is irrelevant. Referencing an unknown interface or provider is a
manifest error naming the declaration and available candidates.

# Provider ownership and selection

The effective platform is the selected SDK plus its optional selected board.
For each required interface, the selected board binding is more specific than
the selected SDK binding.

- A board owns a provider implemented by project-linked board support.
- An SDK owns a default provider supplied by its runtime or external platform
  library.
- A selected board binding for an interface overrides the selected SDK's
  binding for that same interface.
- More than one binding for the same interface in one SDK or one board is a
  manifest error.
- A binding on an unselected SDK or board has no effect on the lane.
- A provider named by several compatible platform definitions remains one
  module with one identity.

Board override is deterministic structural precedence, not declaration order.
It supports the ordinary platform pattern in which an SDK supplies a general
implementation while one board specializes pin multiplexing, UART selection,
clocking, or another facility. Requiring the SDK to withdraw its default for
each specialized board would move board knowledge into the SDK declaration.
Unlike first-match or walk-order selection, most-specific-wins cannot silently
change after a folder reorder.

The current platforms declare:

Opening fence
# platforms/pico/rp2040-ram/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.rp2040_ram.mcu

# platforms/pico/sdk/pico-arm/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.pico.mcu

# platforms/pico/sdk/pico-riscv/mm.mdy
platform-provider: mm.mcu platform.pico.mcu
Closing fence

rp2350-ram and mps2-an385 declare no binding until they have real, tested
implementations. A portable application requiring mm.mcu is unavailable in
those lanes rather than failing late with undefined symbols.

# Provider modules

A provider is an ordinary kind: module with a globally unique name and a use
edge to its interface. Appearing as the right-hand side of any platform-provider
declaration classifies it as a provider target.

A provider target is not an independent root. It is available only when the
selected platform binds its interface to that provider, and it enters an
executable closure only through provider injection. This prevents a root build
from compiling every platform implementation and prevents an unrelated
application from receiving provider static initializers.

The direct-register RP2040 provider has this shape:

Opening fence
mm: 1.1
kind: module
name: rp2040-ram-mcu
module: platform.rp2040_ram.mcu
use: mm.mcu
file: mcu.cppm
option: buildable-host no
Closing fence

It imports mm.mcu, derives from mm::mcu::Platform, implements the facilities the
board supports, and registers one object during static initialization. It uses
the RP2040 register map directly and names no third-party library.

The Pico SDK provider has this shape:

Opening fence
mm: 1.1
kind: module
name: pico-mcu
module: platform.pico.mcu
use: mm.mcu
file: mcu.cppm
option: buildable-host no
Closing fence

It is project-owned platform code, not the public wrapper for a vendored
library. It therefore does not use the lib. module-name prefix and does not
declare library: pico-sdk. Its availability comes from the selected SDK's
platform-provider binding. Beneath its pure C++ interface it may call a private
project-owned C adapter ABI implemented by the Pico external bridge. That ABI is
specific to the bridge and is not the portable mm.mcu contract.

# Pure C++ interface

mm.mcu exports enum class types and a Platform base class. Unsupported remains a
first-class result, so one provider may implement GPIO while inheriting honest
Unsupported answers for UART and time.

The platform partition owns:

- Platform with virtual facility operations and Unsupported defaults;
- set_platform taking a Platform reference;
- platform returning the registered object or an unserved fallback.

The implementation unit owns the fallback object, the current pointer,
set_platform, and platform. Public facility functions call platform and return
typed Status values. An application sees no C declarations, integer status
codes, adapter headers, or vendor types.

The provider owns its subclass and registration object. The provider object is
linked directly, not extracted from an archive, so its namespace-scope
registration initializer is retained. The existing m68k external-link fixture
already proves that project object initializers survive the CMake hand-off.

# Per-executable closure augmentation

Provider requirements are computed before the lane's filtered target tree is
constructed. A shared post-load analysis operates on the complete
project.targets collection and its globally resolved use names:

1. Validate global module identity and the authored use graph.
2. Compute each application and test's transitive platform-interface
   requirements over that unfiltered graph.
3. Resolve the selected SDK defaults and selected board overrides.
4. Record the effective provider, or the reason no provider can be selected,
   for each required interface.
5. Follow each selected provider's authored closure and add any further
   platform interfaces it reaches, repeating to a fixed point.
6. Derive node availability and provider compilation requirements from that
   complete record.
7. Only then construct the filtered build or test tree.

This ordering avoids asking an already filtered tree to discover the dependency
that determines whether a node survives filtering. Build and test use this same
analysis result even though their current root-selection filters differ. The
provider mechanism must not be implemented as separate approximations in
tools/build and tools/test.

The build computes one augmented closure for each executable or test.

1. Compute the ordinary use closure from the requested application or test.
2. Collect every module in that closure declaring platform-interface.
3. Resolve each interface through the selected SDK and board bindings.
4. Validate that the selected provider is available in the lane.
5. Compute the provider's ordinary dependency closure.
6. Repeat from step 2 for interfaces reached only through that provider, until
   no new provider is found.
7. Merge the application and provider closures without repeating targets or
   object paths, preserving the existing authored-closure object order.
8. Append the selected board unit as today.
9. Hand the same final ordered object list to project link or external_link.

Provider closure traversal uses the existing cycle and unknown-module
diagnostics. Fixed-point expansion is independent of manifest order: an
interface reached from a provider is resolved even when its target appeared
earlier in the walk. A provider whose closure does not reach its declared
interface is rejected during project validation rather than linked as an
unrelated object.

Compilation planning must include the selected providers required by any
retained executable. Non-selected providers remain unavailable skipped targets.
A provider is compiled once even when several applications require it, while
its object is added only to each requiring application's link closure.

# Availability behavior

Platform-interface requirements apply at target executable selection, not when
merely loading or compiling the interface module. A host lane binds no target
platform provider and links the interface fallback, allowing a host unit test
to register a stand-in Platform. Applications such as mcu-blink that have no
useful host behavior remain explicitly buildable-host no.

For a target root build, an application whose closure requires an interface
with no selected provider is unavailable and is counted among skipped targets.
Other applications in the lane continue to build. An explicitly requested
target application or test exits with the existing unavailable-target status
and a diagnostic such as:

build: app mcu-blink requires platform interface mm.mcu, but board rp2350-ram and SDK arm-none-eabi-newlib provide no binding

A repeated binding within one SDK or board is a manifest error. A binding on
both the selected SDK and selected board is not ambiguous: the board binding is
the effective one and the SDK binding remains its default.

An application not reaching mm.mcu has no provider requirement even when the
selected platform offers one. target-smoke-any therefore receives no MCU
provider object.

# Core boundary

Core describes the dependencies authored by project code; it is not a promise
that every object in the final platform executable is core. A core executable
may contain non-core runtime or provider objects supplied by its selected
platform. This is already true when a selected SDK supplies newlib, glibc, or an
external platform library without the application declaring that dependency.

Provider injection is likewise a platform edge, not a use declaration authored
by the application. A core application remains core because its authored graph
still cannot declare a third-party library or use an arbitrary non-core module.
For example, platform.pico.mcu may be non-core because it delegates to the Pico
SDK adapter while the portable application importing only core mm.mcu remains
core.

The exemption is narrow:

- the provider must be the exact module named by the selected SDK or board;
- it is injected only for the exact interface named by that binding;
- the provider must depend on that interface;
- it creates no general permission for a core node to use non-core modules;
- a module declaring library remains a lib. wrapper and follows the existing
  core prohibition.

Structural-property validation must evaluate the selected platform edge with
this explicit rule rather than suppressing core checks generally.

# Model

The abstract model exposes the mechanism without selecting arbitrary duplicate
modules.

- ModuleNode exposes whether it carries the platform-interface marker; its
  existing module identity is the interface identity.
- SdkNode and BoardNode expose their platform-provider bindings.
- Platform exposes the effective bindings after SDK and board selection.
- A workflow or artifact query may ask for an application's augmented provider
  closure separately from its authored use closure.

Authored imports remain authored facts. Provider injection is reported as a
platform binding, not inserted into ModuleNode::uses, so model clients can tell
what the application declared from what the lane supplied.

# External CMake bridge

External linking receives the augmented project object list unchanged from both
build and test. The Pico provider object therefore reaches CMake only for
applications and target tests using mm.mcu. Its static initializer is preserved
by the existing EXTERNAL_OBJECT hand-off and direct object ordering.

The bridge continues to compile the private adapter that translates
project-owned C calls into Pico SDK calls. That adapter may remain lane-wide in
the first implementation. A later optimization may pass the required platform
interface set through mm-inputs.cmake and let the bridge omit facility adapters
for executables that do not need them. This optimization is not required for
correct provider selection.

No generic external-builder code names mm.mcu, a provider module, or a Pico
adapter. It transports the final object list and generic bridge inputs only.

# Implemented transition

mm.mcu exports its Platform base class, fallback object, set_platform, and typed
facility dispatch. platform.rp2040_ram.mcu implements GPIO directly against the
RP2040 register map. platform.pico.mcu implements the same C++ interface and
delegates beneath it to the bridge-private Pico adapter ABI. The core interface
contains no C ABI header, and the former lib.mcu identity no longer exists.

mcu-blink imports only mm.mcu. Provider binding makes it available on
rp2040-ram and the Pico SDK lanes, and unavailable on target lanes with no
binding. Unrelated executables retain their ordinary closure.

# Conformance

- The complete project loads with globally unique module names.
- Two different platforms may bind mm.mcu to two different provider modules.
- Two bindings for mm.mcu in one SDK or one board fail before compilation and
  name their owner.
- A selected board binding for mm.mcu overrides a selected SDK default
  independently of manifest declaration and folder order.
- An unknown interface or provider fails at load and names its manifest.
- A provider whose use closure does not reach its interface is rejected.
- An interface reached only through a selected provider is resolved to a fixed
  point; a missing nested provider makes the target unavailable before
  compilation, independently of manifest order.
- mcu-blink names only mm.mcu and remains core.
- mcu-blink links on rp2040-ram through platform.rp2040_ram.mcu.
- With the corresponding external tools installed, mcu-blink links on Pico ARM
  and Pico RISC-V through platform.pico.mcu and the real external CMake bridge.
- An explicit mcu-blink build on rp2350-ram or mps2-an385 reports the missing
  provider before compilation or linking.
- A root build on either unsupported board skips mcu-blink and still builds
  target-smoke-any successfully.
- target-smoke-any contains no provider-module registration object or
  provider-module symbols. A lane-wide external adapter is not mistaken for the
  conditionally injected provider module.
- The mm.mcu implementation object and selected provider appear exactly once in
  a requiring executable's ordered object list.
- Provider static initialization executes in the hosted external-link fixture.
- Project linking and external linking consume identical augmented closure
  semantics.
- The target-mcu-provider compile test receives the direct provider under
  rp2040-ram and delegates through the real external bridge under Pico SDK.
- Model output distinguishes authored use edges from selected provider bindings.
- Host build, all unit suites, check, model, and document pass in a configured
  development environment with the repository's documented host prerequisites.
- Release qualification additionally passes ARM and RISC-V Pico SDK integration
  when the required cross toolchains, Pico SDK checkout, and picotool package
  are available.

# Qualification environments

Unit fixtures for marker grammar, reference resolution, precedence,
availability, closure augmentation, and core validation are mandatory and do
not depend on Pico tools or hardware.

Real Pico ARM and Pico RISC-V links are environment-gated release qualification,
not reasons to weaken those fixtures or to report an implementation failure on
a machine lacking the tools. In this repository workspace the qualification is
currently exercisable: the Pico SDK bundle supplies its picotool package and
the riscv32-pico-elf tools through its bin directory. Other environments must
record a skipped qualification with the missing prerequisite named.

# What this does not settle

- It does not make arbitrary board sources import project modules.
- It does not permit duplicate module names per lane.
- It does not choose individual UART instances, GPIO pin ownership, or clocks.
- It does not promise that every provider supports every mm.mcu facility;
  Unsupported remains part of the interface.
- It does not require a dynamically replaceable provider after program startup.
- It does not yet remove private C linkage at a vendor SDK boundary where the
  SDK itself is C. The portable application and provider contract remain pure
  C++ even when a provider delegates to such an adapter.
